#pragma once

// std::tuple for the supported subset (M10).
//
// The shape is a recursive variadic class template: `tuple<int, char>` is
// `{ int head; tuple<char> tail; }`, and `tuple<>` is the empty primary.
//
// Construction is in place rather than through a factory: a factory returning the
// tuple by value is blocked by a front-end gap, not by the target. KR16 now supports
// aggregate returns (ADR 0048) and a hand-written `std::tuple<int, int>` factory
// works on both targets; see the measured limit below.
//
// Element access is named rather than indexed: the free functions `tuple_head` and
// `tuple_tail`, and the members `.head` / `.tail`. Reach element k by chaining members
// (`pair.tail.head`).
//
// `get<I>` is absent, and the reason was measured rather than assumed. What works: a
// non-type template parameter in a declaration (`Fixed<2> f;`), an explicit non-type
// argument in a call (`at_index<0>(x)`), **deduction** of a non-type parameter from an
// argument (`int size(Fixed<N>&)` called with `Fixed<2>` binds N to 2), and recursion over
// an index (`step<I - 1>(x)` compiles). What does not: a **member function template** fails
// to parse (`expected declarator`), and a qualified use through a template-id with a
// non-type argument now **parses** but does not **resolve** (`Index<0>::pick`,
// `implicit declaration of function`): the parser consumes the template-id as a name
// prefix, so the failure moved into lookup, which does not instantiate the scope. A
// qualified static member works on a plain struct (`S::pick()`) but not through a
// template-id, even via a typedef. A member `get<I>` needs the first. A free `get<I>` has
// recursion available and
// still lacks a **terminator** for `I == 0`: partial specialisation of a function template
// is not legal C++, a function template is not overloadable at all here (templates are
// keyed by bare name, so two declarations differing only in arity collide), and a
// class-template dispatcher needs the qualified use above. The terminator is the whole of
// what is missing.
//
// An empty tuple works as a local and through `tuple_size`: `std::tuple<> empty;
// std::tuple_size(empty);` links and returns zero. It used to emit a call to the partial
// specialisation's pattern constructor, which is only ever declared (ADR 0032), so the
// image failed with `undefined symbol 'tuple_ctor_0_value'`; the call is now skipped for a
// record with no members and no defined constructor. `tuple_size` on an empty pack used to
// fall through to an implicit declaration because deduction could not see the pattern's
// record as an instance; it is now deduced with no arguments.
//
// `make_tuple` is provided. It was blocked by a pack that did not substitute in a
// function template's return type and body; the parser recorded `Tup<Ts...>` in an
// expression context as `Ts ...`, so the pack name carried a trailing space and never
// matched its arity. Fixed on 2026-11-17, together with binding the pack's element types
// (`Ts0`, `Ts1`, ...) and recording a constructor call's resolved type for code
// generation. `make_tuple(1, 2)` compiles and returns the right values on both targets.
// Two-element and three-element tuples both return the right values on both targets; the
// fixture covers both, because a three-element pack exposed a separate defect in the
// signature expansion that a two-element one could not.
// A mixed-element tuple works too: `make_tuple(41, 'a')` returns `tuple<int, char>`. The
// instance name carries each element's type, so a call with `{int, int}` and one with
// `{int, char}` are two instances rather than one -- before that, the second call site
// stored the first one's record type into the other's and the IR verifier refused it.
//
// `make_tuple` handles elements of different types and widths. Every expanded parameter
// takes its own element type, so `make_tuple(41, 'a')` gives `tuple<int, char>` and
// `make_tuple('a', 1000)` keeps the 1000 instead of narrowing it to a char. Returning a
// record by value also carried a defect until now: KR16 lost a struct's trailing
// sub-word member because the hidden destination pointer was pushed before the register
// arguments were loaded, and those loads address staged values relative to SP. That is
// fixed in `kr16_backend.cpp`, and the fixture covers the mixed case on both targets.
//
namespace std {

template <typename... Ts>
struct tuple {
};

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
struct tuple<T, Rest...> {
    T head;
    tuple<Rest...> tail;

    tuple() : head(), tail() {}

    // The element constructor. Its signature carries the class template's pack, so an
    // instance takes one argument per element and `rest...` forwards the remainder to
    // the tail.
    tuple(T first, Rest... rest) : head(first), tail(rest...) {}
};

// The number of elements, for the primary and for every specialization: the pack
// is the whole parameter list, so one function answers both.
template <typename... Ts>
int tuple_size(tuple<Ts...>& value) {
    return sizeof...(Ts);
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
T& tuple_head(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.head;
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
tuple<Rest...>& tuple_tail(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.tail;
}

// The by-value factory. Its signature carries the pack in the return type and in the
// parameter list, which is the case a function template's pack substitutes: the instance
// is named from the arguments and its body builds that instance rather than the pattern.
// `make_tuple(1, 'x')` returns `tuple<int, char>`.
template <typename... Ts>
tuple<Ts...> make_tuple(Ts... values) {
    return tuple<Ts...>(values...);
}

}  // namespace std
